hat's in higher supply is safer. For example, T2 has 4 unshown, so discarding T2 is safe. Similarly, B3 has 4. \n\nIf I discard T2, which is part of the T2-T3-T4 chow, but since it's a high supply tile, maybe it's safe. Alternatively, discard B3, which is part of the B2-B3-B4 chow. \n\nBut I need to maintain the possibility of forming the chows. If I discard T2, then the T2-T3-T4 chow is broken. But if I keep T2, I can keep that chow. So maybe better to discard a tile not part of a chow. \n\nLooking at W1, which is part of a potential chow if I had W2 and W3. But since I don't have W2, maybe it's not useful. Discarding W1 would not affect my chows. \n\nSimilarly, W9 is a terminal, part of the W7-W8-W9 chow. If I discard W9, that chow is broken. But if I keep it, I can have that chow. \n\nGiven that, perhaps the best is to discard a tile that's not part of any chow I can form. For example, W1 or W9. \n\nBut W9 is part of the W7-W8-W9 chow, so if I discard W9, I lose that chow. However, if I keep it, I can have that chow. \n\nAlternatively, if I discard W1, which is not part of any chow I can form, since I don't have W2. \n\nBetween these, maybe discard W1. Because it's not part of any chow, and the unshown count is 3. \n\nBut wait, the valid actions include Play W1. So if I discard W1, it's possible someone could use it to form a chow. But since I don't have W2 or W3 in a sequence with W1, it's unlikely. \n\nAlternatively, maybe discard B9. It's a terminal, and part of a possible B7-B8-B9 chow, but the player across has B5-B6-B7, so maybe they are not using B8-B9. \n\nConsidering all, I think the best move is to discard a tile that's not part of any chow I can form. W1 or W9. Between them, W9 is part of a chow I can form, so keeping it is better. So discard W1. \n\nWait, but the valid action is Play W1. So if I discard W1, it's possible someone could complete a chow with it. But since I don't have W2, it's not part of a chow I can form. So it's safer to discard W1. \n\nTherefore, the best action is Play W1.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The hand has potential for multiple chows and a concealed hand. Discarding W1 preserves key sequences while minimizing risk.\nReason: W1 is not part of any viable chow in the current hand and its unshown count is relatively high (3), reducing the chance of others completing a winning hand. Keeping T2-T3-T4 and B2-B3-B4 chows intact strengthens the possibility of forming four chows with a pair, while maintaining a concealed hand for 4 points.\nAnswer: Play W1"}
